  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 211 PM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\nRest of This Afternoon through Saturday...\n\nThis afternoon, an upper trough was across Missouri into Arkansas. \nMost rain to the south of the area had dissipated. Thicker clouds \ncontinued across the far southern forecast area.\n\nThe upper trough will gradually move northeast through the forecast \narea through Saturday. An isolated shower or thunderstorm is \npossible late this afternoon into this evening across the southern \nforecast area, but better chances for rain will arrive overnight as \nthe upper trough gets closer.\n\nIncreasing mid level winds will lead to increasing isentropic lift \novernight, and this flow will also bring in better moisture. These \nshould be enough for scattered to numerous showers to develop \novernight and continue into Saturday morning. The highest coverage \nwill be across the eastern forecast area where forcing will be \nhighest.\n\nSome thunder is possible, but severe weather is not expected.\n\nSome patchy fog may also form overnight as the atmosphere moistens \nup.\n\nRain chances will diminish from west to east on Saturday afternoon \nas forcing from the upper trough exits.\n\nLows tonight will be in the 60s, with highs on Saturday in the 80s.\n\nSunday and Monday...\n\nGuidance has trended stronger with an upper trough on Sunday, which \nwill send more cooler air into the area behind a front, which should \nnow move south of the area Sunday night. The front will bring \nscattered convection with it, so will have some chance PoPs Sunday \ninto Sunday night.\n\nSome guidance is quite aggressive with the cooler air moving in \nSunday afternoon, with falling temperatures in the late afternoon. \nAm not confident of this occurring though. At any rate, temperatures \nwill peak in the 80s on Sunday.\n\nHigh pressure should keep Monday quiet and cooler. Uncertainties \nremain on just how cool it will be now. NBM 25th and 75th Max T \npercentiles for Indy are 76 and 84 respectively. Will stick close to \nthe mean for now given the uncertainty.\n\nTuesday onward...\n\nThe upper ridge should quickly return north into the area on \nTuesday. This and southwest winds on the west side of exiting high \npressure will allow temperatures to quickly rebound into the upper \n80s to lower 90s for highs Tuesday. \n\nFor Wednesday into Friday, an upper trough well to the north of the \narea will try and push the ridge south and west again. As it does, a \nsurface front will attempt to move south into the area. \n\nGuidance continues to vary in how fast this occurs, and this \nincreases the uncertainty in high temperatures and when the best \nchances for rain will be during this period. Given this uncertainty, \nsee no reason to deviate from blended guidance.\n\nThis results in PoPs Wednesday through Friday, with gradually \ncooling temperatures from the upper 80s/lower 90s Wednesday to upper \n70s/lower 80s by Friday. However, the details will likely change.",
